WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:04.960
Disney's animated Lion King will always hold
a special place in people's hearts.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.150
But the 2019 version takes the opportunity
to correct some of the crazy plot holes, goofs,

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:12.670
and mistakes fans have spotted over the years
in the original.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:17.061
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm explaining how The Lion King

00:00:17.061 --> 00:00:20.640
remake fixes many of those issues from the
90s classic.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.390
Some spoilers ahead, so take care if you haven't
seen it yet.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:27.340
Don't forget my Lion King giveaway is still
running, so be sure to subscribe and leave

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.140
a comment about the movie for your chance
to win.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:34.630
The animated Lion King's opening scene and
'Circle of Life' song are iconic moments that

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:39.630
the remake mostly reproduces shot-for-shot,
however, it also fixes a few crucial errors

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.390
from the 1994 movie.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:46.350
Just before Simba's presentation, Rafiki anoints
him, breaking open a fruit to release a red

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:51.230
liquid which he paints on the young cub's
forehead, before dusting his face to dry the

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.230
mark.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:55.940
The fruit that Rafiki brings with him appears
to come from the ancient baobab tree that

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.510
he lives in, and the problem with the original
scene is that a baobab fruit doesn't contain

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:05.700
a red liquid and is actually dry inside with
white pulp and large seeds.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.920
In the new movie, the filmmakers have fixed
this by having Rafiki use some red roots which

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.439
release a red dust when he breaks them.

00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:16.920
It's also this red powder that makes little
Simba sneeze rather than Rafiki dousing the

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:21.880
poor little cub in a handful of dust as he
did in the animation.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.929
The 2019 movie also changes the hand that
Rafiki uses to anoint Simba from his left

00:01:25.929 --> 00:01:27.450
hand to his right.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:31.179
This is consistent with the fact that Rafiki
is right-handed when he's fighting the hyenas

00:01:31.179 --> 00:01:33.850
with his stick later in both movies.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:39.159
Rafiki himself was misrepresented in the original
movie as, although he's modelled after a mandrill,

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:42.060
in the film and the screenplay he's referred
to as a baboon.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:46.549
Some of the Disney animators even thought
of him as a "mutated" monkey and gave him

00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:50.350
physical features such as a long tail which
is incorrect for a mandrill.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:54.679
"It means you are a baboon, and I'm not."

00:01:54.679 --> 00:01:58.159
"I think you're a little confused."

00:01:58.159 --> 00:01:59.159
"Wrong!"

00:01:59.159 --> 00:02:02.520
That baboon reference has been eliminated
from the new movie and Rafiki's design has

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:07.020
also been corrected, by replacing the long
tail he had in the animation with a short

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:10.540
stub that's biologically accurate for his
species.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:14.599
Another mistake corrected in the new Lion
King is the colour of Simba's eyes.

00:02:14.599 --> 00:02:17.970
In reality, new-born lion cubs start life
with blue-grey eyes.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:22.170
Then after two or three months, a cub's eyes
turn an orangey-brown colour.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:26.629
The remake reflects this as you can see young
Simba has dark-bluish eyes at the time of

00:02:26.629 --> 00:02:28.260
his presentation.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.129
And once he's grown up a little, they're a
darker yellowy-orange colour.

00:02:32.129 --> 00:02:36.819
In the animation, as a new-born cub Simba
simply has dark eyes and when he's older his

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:38.349
eyes are a browny colour.

00:02:38.349 --> 00:02:42.150
And to make us connect with the characters
and their feelings even more, the original

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:47.519
animators gave the lions eyes that were very
like humans, so they have a pupil, an iris

00:02:47.519 --> 00:02:51.220
and a white of their eyes which is sometimes
coloured yellow.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.890
Even though real-life lions don't have whites
in their eyes.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:58.760
Scar's bright green eyes have also been changed
for a better sense of realism in the reboot.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:04.519
The original movie was also guilty of featuring
numerous wild animals that aren't native to

00:03:04.519 --> 00:03:06.170
the film's African setting.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:11.269
For example, when Simba sings "I Just Can't
Wait To Be King", various animals construct

00:03:11.269 --> 00:03:16.010
a crazy tower and on the very top are a group
of giant anteaters.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:20.360
Giant anteaters are only native to Central
and South America and so wouldn't be seen

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:21.890
in the wild in Africa.

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:26.090
So, this time around, the filmmakers have
made sure there's no giant anteaters in the

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:27.090
movie.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:31.510
Other non-African animals that were also scrubbed
from the latest film include the tail-hanging

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:35.659
monkeys that also make an appearance during
"I Just Can't Wait to be King".

00:03:35.659 --> 00:03:39.850
And the gopher that pops up during Zazu's
"morning report" also hit the cutting room

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.079
floor in the remake for the same reason.

00:03:42.079 --> 00:03:43.079
"Zazu."

00:03:43.079 --> 00:03:44.079
"Yes?!"

00:03:44.079 --> 00:03:45.079
"Sir, news from the underground."

00:03:45.079 --> 00:03:49.739
One mistake which has been left in the new
version is the inclusion of leaf-cutter ants.

00:03:49.739 --> 00:03:53.840
These appear in the opening sequence in both
movies even though they're only native to

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:54.840
the Americas.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.579
I think they kept these misplaced ants in
the film for two reasons.

00:03:58.579 --> 00:04:03.659
First of all, the filmmakers wanted to keep
the Circle of Life sequence more or less shot-for-shot.

00:04:03.659 --> 00:04:08.170
And secondly, the ants play a special part
later in Rafiki discovering that Simba is

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:09.840
still alive.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.329
The animated movie often emphasises how dim-witted
the hyenas are:

00:04:14.329 --> 00:04:18.630
"Zazu, you told me they're nothing but slobbering,
mangy, stupid poachers."

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:19.910
"lx-nay on the upid-stay."

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:21.970
"Who are you callin' upid-stay?"

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:26.419
But this doesn't reflect the fact that in
real-life hyenas are intelligent, skilled,

00:04:26.419 --> 00:04:28.050
and dangerous predators.

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:32.460
Which is why Shenzi, who's voiced by Black
Panther's Florence Kasumba, is a lot more

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.020
menacing in the new version.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.440
"You're no match for us Sarabi!

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.210
You will die with your king Nala!"

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:43.759
And although the hyenas are still antagonists
in the remake, the Nazi goose-stepping scene

00:04:43.759 --> 00:04:48.270
has been cut to fit the new film's more realistic
and grounded approach.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:52.770
Hard-core Lion King fans may have noticed
a small change in the new movie when Simba

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.060
and Nala are reunited.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.860
"Where have you been?!"

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:00.470
In the original film, as they rub their heads
together you can also hear them purring, much

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:02.080
like a pet cat does.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:07.470
However, in the remake Simba and Nala's purring
has been removed, likely because animal experts

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:12.400
believe lions either don't purr at all or
the noises they make are technically different

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.810
to the purr of a domestic cat.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:19.430
Following Simba and Nala's reunion is "Can
You Feel The Love Tonight?" which the original

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:24.360
filmmakers described as "perhaps the most
steamy love scene in any Disney film."

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:29.259
During the song, Nala gives Simba what the
filmmakers have called a "Come Hither" look.

00:05:29.259 --> 00:05:34.431
In the new film that moment is replaced with
Nala giving Simba an affectionate lick, which

00:05:34.431 --> 00:05:39.060
fits with the remake's much more realistic
depiction of animal behaviour.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:44.560
In the 1994 movie, Rafiki discovers that Simba
is still alive after the lion flops down on

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:49.310
some plants and the earth and leaves which
scatter are magically carried by the wind

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:54.860
all the way to the wise monkey's tree where
he reads the leaves in an empty tortoise shell.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:59.770
The new movie has a quite ingenious and perhaps
more feasible method for Rafiki to discover

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:01.479
the truth about Simba.

00:06:01.479 --> 00:06:06.199
This time a tuft of Simba's fur falls off
and goes on a complex journey, floating on

00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:10.620
the wind, then a river before being eaten
with some leaves by a giraffe and ending up

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:13.419
in a ball of dung that a beetle rolls along.

00:06:13.419 --> 00:06:18.220
That ball falls down a hill and breaks apart
releasing the tuft of hair, which a leaf-cutter

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:23.030
ant then collects and marches up the Tree
of Life where Rafiki spots it and recognises

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:24.669
it as Simba's fur.

00:06:24.669 --> 00:06:29.210
It's a nice little updated sequence which
also fits with the film's "circle of life"

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:30.210
theme.

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:34.110
In the first film, there's a curious goof
in the scene where Simba and Nala visit the

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:35.160
elephant graveyard.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:38.020
"I wonder if its brains are still in there."

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:39.750
"There's only one way to know.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:41.390
Come on, let's go check it out."

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:46.720
The young lion cubs are ambushed by the hyenas
who emerge from the skull of a dead elephant.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.729
However, fans have commented that when you
look more closely at the proportions of the

00:06:50.729 --> 00:06:56.509
skull, it's grotesquely large and far too
big when compared to real-life elephant skulls.

00:06:56.509 --> 00:07:01.360
Which is why the 2019 movie skips over the
hyenas coming out of the skulls, and instead

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.810
they meet Simba and Nala inside a cave.

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:09.650
Another problem fixed from the animated Circle
of Life sequence is Rafiki's seemingly impossible

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.889
ascent to Pride Rock from the front.

00:07:11.889 --> 00:07:16.129
If you look at the shape of Pride Rock and
how it juts out, it would be extremely difficult

00:07:16.129 --> 00:07:20.910
for Rafiki to climb up beneath it and very
silly too given he could access the rock via

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:23.169
a much easier route instead.

00:07:23.169 --> 00:07:28.810
The new movie fixes with this by showing Rafiki
walking on to the top via a much more gently-sloping

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:29.919
rock face.

00:07:29.919 --> 00:07:35.660
As you can see, the new Lion King tends to
tone down some of the animation's more exaggerated,

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:39.789
cartoony moments to better fit the photo-realism
of the new CG version.

00:07:39.789 --> 00:07:40.789
"What is it?"

00:07:40.789 --> 00:07:42.050
"That is not a lion, it's a furry bird!"

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:43.050
"Looks like a lion."

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:44.710
"Th.. is not a lion.

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:46.169
Let me get a closer look."

00:07:46.169 --> 00:07:50.870
So, the remake changes this moment where Timon
lifts Simba's paw up all the way above his

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:53.760
head in an incredible feat of strength for
a tiny little meerkat.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:59.400
Instead, the new movie, has Timon identifying
Simba as a lion by looking at his teeth.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:00.400
"It's a lion!

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:01.400
Run for your life Pumbaa!"

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:02.660
"Hey Timon, wait, wait, wait, Timon!

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:05.240
Wait, it's a little lion!"

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:06.740
"It gets bigger!"

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:10.960
Another scene made more realistic this time
around is when Nala chases Pumbaa.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:15.289
In the original movie, the warthog rather
foolishly tries to escape her by squeezing

00:08:15.289 --> 00:08:18.110
through a gap under a tree root that's too
small for him.

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:19.470
"How bleak is that?"

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:23.439
Which is why it doesn't happen like that in
the new story, and instead Pumbaa is cornered

00:08:23.439 --> 00:08:28.220
by Nala when he slips and falls trying to
run up a moss-covered rock.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:32.370
Another small but important change, which
die-hard Lion King fans may have noticed,

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:37.830
is that after Scar is defeated and Simba walks
out on to Pride Rock, in the remake Zazu says

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:39.360
"your majesty".

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:45.529
It's a nice fix to the 1994 ending where Zazu
says these words to Simba but the actual audio

00:08:45.529 --> 00:08:47.820
ended up cut from the final film.

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:53.459
If you watch the animated scene closely, you'll
see Zazu mouth the words as Simba walks past,

00:08:53.459 --> 00:08:57.370
but the dialogue can't be heard.

00:08:57.370 --> 00:09:01.800
That mistake was introduced because the filmmakers
thought a moment of silence would add more

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:03.330
gravitas to the scene.

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:07.589
However, they didn't redo the animation to
stop Zazu's mouth moving.

00:09:07.589 --> 00:09:11.890
Speaking of adding things to the film, the
new movie also adds some new meta moments

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:16.600
and Disney easter eggs in the Timon and Pumbaa
scenes, and I talk all about that in my Lion

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:18.180
King comparison video.

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:21.560
Tap the screen to watch that or click the
link in the video description.

